SparkCPIO's loadheader: newc's and crc's 8 hex digits end before strtoul.

For the ASCII headers loadheader reads each field into temp[16] and converts
it with strtoul.  The octal cases end the digits (temp[6]=0, temp[11]=0); the
hex case ('x': newc, crc) does not, so the bytes after the 8 digits -- what
was last on the stack there -- are read as more digits when they happen to be
hex.  RISC OS 5.30's SparkFS then calls a newc archive "Bad archive", once
something (an odc archive's 11 octal digits, for one) has left digits there,
until a reset.  The 8 digits are now ended, as the other cases end theirs.

diff -ru a/SparkCPIO/cat.c b/SparkCPIO/cat.c
--- a/SparkCPIO/cat.c	2026-10-02 07:42:26.730995919 +0100
+++ b/SparkCPIO/cat.c	2026-10-02 07:42:26.746836749 +0100
@@ -463,6 +463,7 @@
    case 'x':
             err=readarc(arc,temp,8);
             if(err) break;
+            temp[8]=0;                   /* as 'o' and 'O' end theirs */
             val=strtoul(temp,0,16);
             hdr->array[o]=val;
             break;
